📌 Normal subquery → runs once, gives result → outer query uses it.
📌 Correlated subquery → runs again and again for each row in outer query.

Real-Life Analogy
Imagine you are a bank auditor checking:

"Which customers made a transaction more than their own average transaction amount?"

You look at one customer → check their transactions → decide yes/no → go to next customer → repeat.

The checking part (inner query) depends on which customer (outer query row) you are looking at.

2. Flow of a Correlated Subquery
Let’s go step-by-step.

We have two tables:

CUSTOMERS

CUSTOMER_ID	NAME
101	Rajesh
102	Anjali
103	Suresh
TRANSACTIONS

TXN_ID	CUSTOMER_ID	AMOUNT
1	101	5000
2	101	7000
3	102	15000
4	102	18000
5	103	4000
6	103	3000
Query: Find customers who have any transaction above their own average transaction amount
SELECT DISTINCT c.CUSTOMER_ID, c.NAME
FROM CUSTOMERS c
WHERE EXISTS (
    SELECT 1
    FROM TRANSACTIONS t
    WHERE t.CUSTOMER_ID = c.CUSTOMER_ID
      AND t.AMOUNT > (
          SELECT AVG(AMOUNT)
          FROM TRANSACTIONS
          WHERE CUSTOMER_ID = c.CUSTOMER_ID
      )
);
