Outer Query picks first row → Rajesh (CUSTOMER_ID = 101).

Go to subquery:

t.CUSTOMER_ID = c.CUSTOMER_ID means → only Rajesh’s transactions.

Calculate Rajesh’s average amount → (5000 + 7000) / 2 = 6000.

Check if any transaction > 6000 → yes, 7000 is greater.

Result → Rajesh included.

Outer Query picks second row → Anjali (102).

Average = (15000 + 18000) / 2 = 16500.

Transaction > 16500? Yes, 18000.

Result → Anjali included.

Outer Query picks third row → Suresh (103).

Average = (4000 + 3000) / 2 = 3500.

Transaction > 3500? Yes, 4000.

Result → Suresh included.

3. Key Points
The inner query uses outer query columns (c.CUSTOMER_ID in this case).

This makes it correlated — the subquery changes depending on outer query’s current row.

Performance can be slower for large datasets, because it runs many times.

